WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
Ras has no segment crossing the membrane, yet it works at the membrane's inner face.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.540
What keeps it there?

3
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.840
The puzzle has two parts.

4
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.220
A lipid must join the protein, and that lipid must remain in a bilayer.

5
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:22.553
First, separate two interactions that students often mix up.

6
00:00:23.673 --> 00:00:26.593
A covalent bond joins the lipid to the protein.

7
00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:32.473
A much weaker, non-covalent contact holds the lipid tail in the bilayer.

8
00:00:33.713 --> 00:00:38.673
The covalent bond can last while the tail repeatedly enters and leaves membranes.

9
00:00:38.673 --> 00:00:43.953
Permanent attachment to Ras does not mean permanent membrane binding.

10
00:00:45.017 --> 00:00:48.777
Watch one farnesyl tail sample the membrane and release.

11
00:00:50.177 --> 00:00:53.497
What should happen if Ras gains a compatible second contact?

12
00:00:54.817 --> 00:00:57.657
Will its visits remain brief, or become much longer?

13
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:05.263
The visits become longer.

14
00:01:06.603 --> 00:01:10.143
Both contacts must release together before the protein escapes.

15
00:01:11.463 --> 00:01:13.123
That coincidence is rarer.

16
00:01:14.203 --> 00:01:16.543
A second lipid can provide that contact.

17
00:01:17.803 --> 00:01:22.583
So can a positive patch attracted to negative lipids on the membrane's inner face.

18
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:27.447
H-Ras and N-Ras use farnesyl plus palmitate.

19
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:32.767
K-Ras4B uses farnesyl plus a lysine-rich positive patch.

20
00:01:34.107 --> 00:01:36.647
In both, farnesyl supplies the first contact.

21
00:01:38.007 --> 00:01:39.827
These are not identical addresses.

22
00:01:41.147 --> 00:01:44.847
They are two ways to combine membrane contact with another signal.

23
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.550
Why is the change so large?

24
00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.030
Favorable free-energy contributions add.

25
00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:56.150
The equilibrium effect grows exponentially, not step by step.

26
00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.790
This is a thermodynamic effect, not a new covalent bond.

27
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:07.590
A computed curve shows how a modest extra contribution can greatly raise the share

28
00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.730
of time on the membrane.

29
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:12.673
The second contact also creates control.

30
00:02:13.993 --> 00:02:18.813
Remove palmitate, or weaken a positive patch, and the strong hold becomes weak

31
00:02:18.813 --> 00:02:21.813
again. That is a switch in location.

32
00:02:23.193 --> 00:02:27.193
The protein can leave without breaking its permanent farnesyl attachment.

33
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:33.197
Palmitate is especially useful because cells can remove and replace it.

34
00:02:34.297 --> 00:02:38.217
It goes on mainly at the Golgi, and it can come off on any membrane.

35
00:02:39.717 --> 00:02:45.017
Farnesyl remains attached while palmitate changes whether H-Ras is tightly trapped

36
00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:47.057
or loosely sampling membranes.

37
00:02:48.457 --> 00:02:52.117
The same protein changes residence without losing farnesyl.

38
00:02:53.117 --> 00:02:55.457
This is the grip half of the lesson's rule.

39
00:02:56.957 --> 00:03:01.957
Lipids control signaling protein location by supplying grip, address, or both.

40
00:03:03.257 --> 00:03:06.057
The rule applies within this membrane-targeting scope.

41
00:03:07.297 --> 00:03:12.097
Here, the second signal converts a brief encounter into regulated membrane

42
00:03:12.097 --> 00:03:13.097
residence.

43
00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.510
So ask three questions about any lipid-modified protein.

44
00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:20.530
Which contact puts it on membranes?

45
00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:25.270
Which additional signal strengthens or directs that contact?

46
00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.370
And what can remove or oppose it?

47
00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.650
The answers predict both localization and the switch that can change it.
